Original Concept & Authorship Notice
─────────────────────────────────────
The OoruShare concept — decentralised residential driveway sharing as authorised
2-minute delivery loading bays, coordinated by an autonomous agentic curb governor,
governed by Resident Welfare Associations (RWAs), and monetised via Business
Associate Agreements (BAA) with quick-commerce dark store operators — was conceived,
designed, and built from scratch by Pankajkr Jain (Pankaj Kumar) in November 2026
for the IBM Bobathon: Build for Bengaluru hackathon (Theme 1: Smarter Streets
Better Living).

Originality Anchors (verifiable via git commit history):
  - First commit: ff1b36d  (Nov 2026 — IBM Bobathon submission origin)
  - Repository:   https://github.com/PankajKumar2804/oorushare
  - Builder:      Pankajkr Jain  ·  SWE AI Full Stack  ·  Healthcare Industry
  - AI leverage:  IBM Bob (execution multiplier — not the idea originator)

Specific IP elements originated by Pankajkr Jain:
  1. The "Last 10 Metres" framing of the quick-commerce residential delivery gap
  2. The RSU (Resident Spot Unit) — atomic unit of idle driveway capacity
  3. The 4-sided marketplace: resident · rider · dark store · RWA
  4. BAA (Business Associate Agreement) enterprise monetisation model
  5. Community credit system (10 credits = ₹50 RWA maintenance offset)
  6. Agentic curb governor with IBM Granite 3.8B time-series dispatch staggering
  7. PostgreSQL advisory lock atomic 2-minute bay reservation pattern
  8. RWA human-in-the-loop governance gate for driveway activation
  9. Civic data licensing to BBMP/BTP as zero-marginal-cost revenue stream
 10. The principle that no single quick-commerce platform will build this
     because it benefits all of them equally — requiring a neutral civic layer

